{"candidates":[{"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts following an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single expert because it replaces a single point of failure (the expert's cognitive bias/fatigue) with a redundant, distributed verification system that minimizes 'type 1' errors.","counterconditions":["The task requires 'synthetic' judgment where the whole is greater than the sum of the checklist parts.","The checklist is poorly designed, introducing new systemic biases that override the benefit of redundancy."],"mechanism":"The checklist decomposes a complex intuitive leap into a series of binary, verifiable sub-tasks. While the expert uses System 1 (fast, heuristic), the panel uses System 2 (slow, analytical). Reliability emerges from the intersection of multiple non-expert observers; if the checklist is well-constructed, the probability of all panel members missing a critical 'false' flag is lower than the probability of a single expert having a blind spot.","optional_refs":["SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"The single expert remains more reliable because non-experts lack the 'semantic depth' to correctly interpret checklist items in edge cases, leading to 'brittle compliance' where the right answer is reached for the wrong reasons.","counterconditions":["The task is highly algorithmic with zero ambiguity in item interpretation.","The checklist includes exhaustive 'if-then' logic for all known edge cases."],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is actually highly compressed pattern recognition. A checklist provides the 'what' but not the 'why'. In complex tasks, non-experts will treat checklist items as literal strings rather than conceptual requirements, failing to recognize when a specific case renders a checklist item irrelevant or misleading.","optional_refs":["SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"disanalogies":["Standard reliability tests usually look at aggregate outcomes, whereas this requires granular item-level auditing."],"overturn_conditions":["Evidence that 'local accuracy' does not correlate with 'outcome reliability' in non-experts."],"shared_structure":["Introduction of a 'trap' or perturbation to test the integrity of the process.","Distinction between superficial alignment (outcome) and structural alignment (process)."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001","SCR_002"],"transfer_claims":["The 'Trap Item' method can distinguish between a cognitive crutch and a rigorous filter."]},"claim":"Genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere compliance by the presence of 'active dissent' on trap items—specifically, the panel's ability to mark a checklist item as 'False' even when the overall outcome feels 'True'.","counterconditions":["The panel is incentivized to reach a consensus outcome regardless of intermediate steps.","The checklist items are so loosely defined that 'False' markers are dismissed as user error."],"mechanism":"If a panel is merely complying, they will 'rubber stamp' the checklist to reach the expected conclusion. If they are reliable, they will exhibit 'local accuracy'—correctly identifying a failed criterion even if they suspect the final answer is correct. The delta between 'item-level accuracy' and 'outcome-level accuracy' reveals whether the checklist is a rigorous filter or a performative ritual.","sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Reliability is a function of 'error-correlation'; the panel wins only if their errors are uncorrelated, whereas the expert's errors are systemic to their specific mental model.","counterconditions":["The checklist itself introduces a shared, correlated error across the entire panel.","The expert's model is more accurate than the checklist's logic."],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is a single, coherent model. If that model is wrong about a specific 